"A bit is either 0 or 1, and nothing in between.": "da8e4b50ea58",
"A row of bits can say much more. Think about how we normally count. We have ten digits, 0 to 9. When we run out, we carry into a new place, and each place is worth ten times the one to its right: ones, tens, hundreds. Binary works the same way with only two digits, so each place is worth two times the one to its right: 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, and so on. To read a row of switches, add up the places that are on. The row 1101 means 8 + 4 + 1, which is 13.": "31c479fd3089",
"Counting up is simple, too. To add one, flip the switch on the far right. If it was off, it turns on, and you are done. If it was already on, it turns off and passes a carry to the switch on its left, which does the same thing. The carry can run down the row like falling dominoes: 0111 is 7, and one more makes 1000, which is 8. With eight switches you can count from 0 to 255. Eight bits together are called a byte. Every switch you add doubles how far you can count.": "83ae82e6fbfd",
"Electrical noise made the old radio hiss.": "7b38a3dd4f88",
"Flip the switch by the door to turn on the light.": "85121d8996cb",
"How do you find the number that a row of switches shows?": "82e23fb62a20",
"In binary counting, there are only two digits: 0 and 1.": "a1242aaaee55",
"One byte is made of eight bits.": "813adc5cf23a",
"One switch holds the smallest piece of information there is: a single 0 or 1. It is called a bit, which is short for “binary digit.” Binary means “made of two parts.” The mathematician John Tukey suggested the word, and Claude Shannon used it in 1948 in a famous paper about sending messages. One bit cannot say much. It can answer only one yes-or-no question: Is the light on? Is the door open?": "3b10a99b5e0f",
"The idea is much older than computers. The German thinker Gottfried Leibniz had worked out binary numbers by 1679, and in 1703 he wrote an article explaining how to do arithmetic with only 0 and 1. In it he also described something a French missionary in Beijing, Joachim Bouvet, had noticed: the six-line figures in the ancient Chinese Book of Changes match binary numbers, if a whole line is read as 1 and a broken line as 0. Much later, as a student at MIT, Claude Shannon showed that circuits made of switches could carry out logic. Today those switches are transistors on a chip. They are tiny and fast, but each one still does what the lights in the model do: off or on, 0 or 1.": "ae872c5bce86",
"The number 255 has three digits.": "8e23ad2dc9bc",
"The phone lost its signal inside the tunnel.": "45127fb12919",
"What is a bit, and what two values can it have?": "0fa3acba4c82",
"When you add 9 and 1, you carry a one into the tens place.": "a4a463e5b6fd",
"Why is it safer for a computer to use two levels of electricity than ten?": "78ad01236c9a",
"Why two states and not ten? A computer could try to use ten levels of voltage, one for each digit. But electrical signals are never perfectly steady. There is always a little unwanted wobble, called noise. With ten levels packed close together, a small wobble could make a 6 look like a 7. With only two levels, far apart, the signal has to change a lot before off could be mistaken for on. Engineers have tried other ways: ENIAC, an early American computer, counted in tens, and a Soviet computer called Setun used three states. Nearly every computer today uses two.": "8b4fd7ccd3ef",
"Your phone or computer can show photos, play songs, and send messages around the world. But if you could look deep inside its chips, you would not find any pictures, sounds, or letters. You would find switches: billions of them on a single chip, each one far too small to see. Every switch can be in only one of two states, off or on. We write off as 0 and on as 1. Everything a computer does, from games to video calls, is built out of these two digits.": "adb48af1d5be",
